# Build outputs.  The rule is simple: nothing that a build produces is tracked,
# except the two bootstrap artefacts the build needs in order to start.
#
#   selfhost/build/vm.c   the seed C the toolchain bootstraps vm.exe from, and
#   selfhost/build/vm.exe the compiler itself
#
# `vm.c` is generated — the fixpoint regenerates and byte-compares it — but
# without it there is nothing to bootstrap from, so it is the one generated file
# that is tracked.  `vm.exe` is tracked too, so a fresh checkout can run the
# compiler without a build step; it is promoted by `tools\build.ps1`.

# compiler and program outputs
*.obj
*.exe
*.dll
*.pdb
*.ilk
*.lib
*.exp
*.o

# `*.dll` earns its own paragraph.  `tools\build.ps1` copies `LLVM-C.dll`
# (74,159,616 B) beside the compiler on purpose -- the compiler is what needs it
# at run time, and that is the plan's step 4 -- but this list originally covered
# `.obj`, `.exe`, `.lib`, `.pdb` and `.ilk` and said nothing about `.dll`.  So the
# copy turned up in `git status` as one `git add -A` away from a public
# repository, where a 74 MB blob is in the history of every clone forever and
# deleting it later does not take it out.  The rule is not left to memory:
# `tools\check-hygiene.ps1` asks git about every untracked file over 5 MB and
# fails, naming the ones git does not ignore.

# the emitted C, wherever it lands: beside a source in the corpus, in the
# benchmarks, in the examples, or in the repository root by accident.
#
# The pattern has to name what is *generated*, not the directory it lands in.
# The first version of this file ignored `/tests/build/**`, which is where 65 of
# the corpus's `.vel` sources live: a fresh checkout would have lost most of the
# test suite while looking perfectly healthy, and three of those sources have
# already been deleted once in this project's history and could not be recovered.
/hello.c
/examples/*.c
/ide-demo/*.c
/bench/*.c
/selfhost/*.c
/selfhost/llvm/*.c
# ... except the ones a person wrote.  `phase2_spike.c` is hand-written evidence
# that the in-process LLVM path works, not output from a build, and the blanket
# rule above swallowed it once already.
!/selfhost/llvm/phase2_spike.c
/tests/probes/*.c
/runtime/_*.c
/tests/build/**/*.c
/tests/build/**/*.obj
/tests/build/**/*.exe
/bench/build/**
/examples/build/**

# what a fresh checkout needs in order to start at all, and the only generated
# files that are tracked: the seed C the compiler bootstraps from and the
# promoted compiler binary itself
!/selfhost/build/vm.c
!/selfhost/build/vm.exe

# the suite's own captures and work files
/tests/build/_cap_*
/tests/build/_rec*
/tests/run_tests.exe
/tests/run_tests.obj

# the linked compiler source and its backup.  `tools/link_selfhost.vel` builds
# `selfhost/vm.vel` out of `selfhost/vela.vel` plus `selfhost/parts/*.vel`, and
# `selfhost/vm.vel.before-link` is what it keeps for diagnosis.  Both are products of
# a 400 KB concatenation, so tracking them turns every edit to a part into a diff of
# the whole compiler; `tools\build.ps1` step 2 regenerates them before anything reads
# them.
/selfhost/vm.vel
/selfhost/vm.vel.before-link

# the compiler's build directory: keep vm.c and promote results, ignore the rest
/selfhost/build/*.obj
/selfhost/build/_*
/selfhost/build/*.ilk
/selfhost/build/*.pdb
/tools/link_selfhost.exe
/tools/link_selfhost.obj
/tools/_*

# the plugin's build tree and packages
/idea-plugin/build/**
/idea-plugin/dist/**
/idea-plugin/out/**

# scratch directories agents work in, inside the checkout
/_probe_lang/**
/_cwdtest/**
/_feat/**
/build/**
/extern_scratch/**
/scratch/**

# the tools' own work directories: refreeze's golden backup and smoke's scratch
/.work/**

# generated C inside the compiler's own build directory, except the seed
/selfhost/build/*.c
!/selfhost/build/vm.c
/tests/run_tests.c

# the C-compiler driver's own scratch, which `tools\build.ps1` writes for
# diagnosability: `vm.exe_cc.bat` and `vm.exe_cc_cmd.txt` are regenerated on every
# build, and two of them were committed in the first commit by accident.  The rule
# below did not catch the pair written one directory up — measured 2026-09-20,
# `selfhost/vm.exe_cc.bat` (531 B, holding an absolute path into a `%TEMP%`
# directory that no longer exists) was still tracked while every build recreated
# it, and its `_cmd.txt` sibling had zero references anywhere.  Both spellings are
# covered now, and `tools\check-hygiene.ps1` is what asks the question again.
/selfhost/build/*.bat
/selfhost/build/*.txt
/selfhost/*.bat
/selfhost/*.txt

# a debugger session's command files: `tools\dbg_test.ps1` rebuilds the directory
# on every run, so whatever is left in it is a previous run's transcript rather
# than a fixture.  Two were tracked.
/tools/dbg-session/

# agents' scratch files: `_`-prefixed, wherever they were working.  These are
# not evidence and not source -- 45 files named `_ovf_child_NN.txt` from one
# overflow study had been sitting in `runtime\` since the day it ran.
/_*
/runtime/_*
/tools/_*
/selfhost/_*

# the build report, written next to the checkout by tools\build.ps1
/../vela-build-report.txt

# editor/OS noise
.vscode/
*.swp
*~
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
